{"id": "shame_001", "category": "Emotional Regulation", "subcategory": "Shame Resilience", "content_type": "affirmation", "title": "The Fleeting Spotlight", "affirmation": "This moment does not define my worth. People move on quickly, and I allow myself to do the same.", "situation": null, "tone": "gentle", "purpose": "To reduce self-judgment and the feeling of being constantly scrutinized after a public error.", "duration_minutes": 1, "practice_steps": [], "reflection_questions": [], "journaling_prompt": "How does it feel to realize that others are likely preoccupied with their own challenges?", "closing_message": "You are more than this single moment of imperfection.", "tags": ["shame", "self-compassion", "perspective"]}
{"id": "shame_002", "category": "Mindfulness", "subcategory": "Grounding", "content_type": "practice", "title": "The Time-Travel Perspective", "affirmation": null, "situation": null, "tone": "grounding", "purpose": "To manage physical symptoms of shame without suppression and visualize the mistake as a distant memory.", "duration_minutes": 5, "practice_steps": ["Notice where you feel shame in your body and breathe into that space without trying to change it.", "Close your eyes and imagine yourself one year from today, asking if this specific moment still impacts your daily happiness.", "Gently shift your attention to the present task, reminding yourself that feelings are temporary visitors."], "reflection_questions": ["What is the actual evidence that people are still thinking about this?", "Will this event matter in one month, or in five years?"], "journaling_prompt": "Write a letter to your future self about how you navigated this moment of discomfort.", "closing_message": "Time has a way of softening the edges of our errors.", "tags": ["mindfulness", "somatic", "future-self"]}
{"id": "shame_003", "category": "Cognitive Reframing", "subcategory": "Social Anxiety", "content_type": "situation_based", "title": "Navigating the Aftermath", "affirmation": null, "situation": "Re-entering a social space where people witnessed the mistake or recalling the event and feeling your face flush.", "tone": "realistic", "purpose": "To challenge the spotlight effect and manage the anxiety of recurring thoughts of embarrassment.", "duration_minutes": 10, "practice_steps": ["Acknowledge the memory without fighting it, taking three slow, deep breaths before entering the space.", "Recall a mistake a friend made recently and notice how little you remember about it, applying that same logic to yourself.", "Identify one person in the room who is generally kind or neutral to focus on."], "reflection_questions": ["Am I reacting to current events or to my memory of the event?", "How much do I judge others compared to myself?"], "journaling_prompt": "Describe one small step you can take to re-engage socially today, and write down three times someone else's mistake barely impacted you.", "closing_message": "Attention shifts quickly, and you can move forward even while feeling uncomfortable.", "tags": ["social-recovery", "courage", "spotlight-effect"]}

{"id":"ldr-comm-25-001","category":"Relationships","subcategory":"Long-Distance Communication","content_type":"situation_based","title":"Grounding Yourself Before Reaching Out","affirmation":"My worth is steady and complete, regardless of how quickly someone replies to my messages today.","situation":"Feeling insecure or neglected due to delayed responses or lack of contact in a long-distance relationship.","tone":"grounding","purpose":"To regulate emotions, reinforce self-worth, and facilitate clear, non-accusatory communication about needs.","duration_minutes":15,"practice_steps":["Pause and take three deep breaths to ground yourself in the present moment.","Identify the specific emotion you are feeling without judging it as wrong.","Describe the behavior without judgment, such as noting that you haven't spoken in two days.","Draft your message using an 'I feel' statement, focusing on connection rather than blame.","Review your draft to ensure it states feelings and needs, not accusations."],"reflection_questions":["What evidence do I have that I am loved versus evidence for my insecurity?","Am I reacting to the present situation or past hurts?","How can I comfort myself right now independent of my partner?"],"journaling_prompt":"Write a draft of your request using the formula: 'I feel [emotion] when [behavior], and I would appreciate [specific action]. Then, list three things that make you feel secure within yourself.","closing_message":"Your feelings are valid, and clear communication starts with inner stability. It is okay to feel anxious; the goal is to move through it with kindness toward yourself.","tags":["LDR","communication","self-worth","anxiety","boundaries","self-regulation"]}
