{"id": "focus-restore-001", "category": "Mental Wellness", "subcategory": "Focus & Attention", "content_type": "practice", "title": "Tech Interruption Reset Protocol", "affirmation": "It is okay that my focus was broken; I can gently guide it back without criticizing myself.", "situation": "Feeling overwhelmed by a sudden influx of messages during a high-interruption work block in a tech environment.", "tone": "grounding", "purpose": "To restore cognitive bandwidth, reduce context-switching fatigue, and minimize the emotional tax of interruptions.", "duration_minutes": 5, "practice_steps": ["Pause and notice the interruption or fatigue without labeling it as failure.", "Take three physiological sighs to reset your nervous system arousal.", "Name three physical objects in your immediate environment to anchor yourself.", "Write down the previous task context briefly to clear mental clutter.", "Scan any incoming messages and categorize them into 'urgent' and 'can wait'.", "Address only the most urgent item, then choose one small next action to re-engage."], "reflection_questions": ["What physical sensations arise when I am interrupted?", "How does self-judgment affect my return to focus?", "Did the urgency of these messages match my internal feeling of panic?"], "journaling_prompt": "Describe a recent interruption and the feeling of 'mental fog' that followed. How might you respond differently using this reset protocol?", "closing_message": "Progress is built on returning, not perfect continuity. Be patient with your brain as it re-engages.", "tags": ["focus", "mindfulness", "tech-wellness", "neuroscience", "self-compassion"]}
