{"id": "bw_anxiety_001", "category": "Mental Health", "subcategory": "Anxiety Regulation", "content_type": "practice", "title": "Physiological Sigh and Somatic Grounding Routine", "affirmation": "My breath anchors me in the present moment, allowing space for feelings without demanding immediate solutions.", "situation": "When experiencing heightened anxiety, physical restlessness, or a mounting to-do list.", "tone": "grounding", "purpose": "Regulate autonomic nervous system response and cultivate somatic awareness during acute stress.", "duration_minutes": 7, "practice_steps": ["Find a comfortable seated position with your spine neutral and feet flat on the floor.", "Inhale deeply through your nose until your lungs feel nearly full.", "Take a second, shorter sip of air on top of the first inhale to fully expand the alveoli.", "Exhale slowly through your mouth with a long, audible sigh, releasing tension.", "Place one hand on your belly and one on your heart, noticing the physical support beneath you.", "Gently name three things you can see and two things you hear, returning focus to your breath whenever the mind wanders."], "reflection_questions": ["What physical sensations were most prominent, and where did tension release?", "Did the intensity or pace of your anxiety shift throughout the practice?", "Which sensory observation felt most grounding?"], "journaling_prompt": "Describe where you felt anxiety in your body before and after this routine, and how the tension shifted.", "closing_message": "You do not need to fix everything right now; just breathing is enough.", "tags": ["breathwork", "anxiety", "somatic", "grounding", "nervous-system"]}
